		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Smith radically transformed economic thought, laying the foundations for policies that would produce unprecedented increases in productivity and wealth. By the eighteenth century, much of the emerging [[World economy|world economy]] was dominated by the [[Feudalism|feudal]] and [[Mercantilism|mercantilist]] institutions of the [[Europe|European]] powers. In Europe itself, feudalism still shaped economic life through [[Nobility|hereditary privilege]], [[Guild|guilds]], and restrictions on labour and commerce, while internationally, [[Mercantilism|mercantilism]] sought to increase national wealth through state intervention, [[Monopoly|colonial trading monopolies]], protective tariffs, and restrictions on international trade.",
